lear it's for us to play a major  and leading role the world expects us to  be a point of reference a piece builder  a security provider and a voice of  rationality I would say sometimes even  common sense in a very difficult word in  the various last months I've heard this  call at all the corners of the world  just a couple of examples in Mozambique  where it was for the signing of an  historic peace deal just a couple of  weeks ago because we have followed the  negotiations all through these five  years and they are now asking us to be  close to them to help them implement  this final agreement and win the piece  that sometimes is more difficult than  making the agreement itself in Southeast  Asia or in Ukraine in Africa or in the  Gulf what I've heard in this recent  months all our partners asking us to  keep engaging with them as we've done in  these years to keep supporting mediation  free and fair trade or the fight against  climate change and these are just  examples of why people see in us our  partners Cena's a vital point of  reference the world needs us today and  it is in our interest to keep a  consistent and very strong engagement in  world affairs in every single region of  the world I know we often have the  feeling at least I do and I guess you  might have that feeling as well that the  world is going in the wrong direction  that everything becomes more difficult  every single day that goes by and I  guess this is not just an impression  sometimes developments we're seeing on  the ground or in word your politics are  the ones we wish to see and sometimes  are not the ones we work for sometimes  you manage to achieve results sometimes  you don't my natural reaction in this  situations it's because I'm an optimist  otherwise you don't manage to make this  this work I think it's a in the job  description is imagine what if we didn't  work for achieving a better result it  could have been even worse I don't know  if this is an optimistic or pessimistic  approach but anyway I think this is what  can guide us in in our daily work we  always try to give a positive  contribution even when we have the  feeling that the world is going in a  wrong direction because our actions do  make a difference sometimes they don't  make the headlines and sometimes it's  even good they don't make the headlines  because sometimes you have to exercise  quiet diplomacy sometimes it will only  bear fruit after several years and  perseverance and patience is what we  need in many occasions but what we do  has always an impact on thousands of  people and I think you and the teams you  work with in the delegation so you will  work within the delegations are the  first ones to see this the impact that  our work has on daily lives of the  people we meet on a daily basis and the  work we do is always key and sometimes  vital to so many of our partners around  the world  because the more our work becomes  difficult the more our partners need us  to be at their site on the path of  cooperation and the difficult search of  win-win solutions I imagine that has  happened to many of you that have been  ahead of the legation already for some  time to hear this  this sentence that I've heard so often  especially in the last few months a few  years we stand on this position but  obviously if you have the same position  it's easier for us to keep it this is a  request that we hear from many corners  of the world  and this is why I believe we need to be  aware of the power we have as European  Union that is again  sufficient alone but vital for many  others that seen as a point of reference  simply because we're bigger than most of  our partners was stronger than most of  our partners in the world so today I  will try to focus on a few success  stories but also on a few lessons  learned of this last year's the first  one is on security and defence the  lesson I've learned here is that as an  excellent Mandela said it always seems  impossible until it's 